  "worked_solution": "- The complex number $-2-2\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-2$ and the imaginary part $y=-2.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-2,-2)$, which lies in the third quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-2+\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-2$ and the imaginary part $y=1.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-2,1)$, which lies in the second quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-1+2\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-1$ and the imaginary part $y=2.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-1,2)$, which lies in the second quadrant.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035975/assets/9f2e7234ba52d14098e0ecfc9695181b425764c110995b76b4b0efddf206252c.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
